I have an update...I couldn't at the time figure out what was up as VS2019 had no module with debug symbols for System.Data.ODBC. However, VS2022 does :-)

From System.Data.ODBC, there is an exception thrown ('System.OverflowException' in System.Data.dll) when trying to set SQL_COLUMN_SCALE to -1 (see above for some of the details about that). The exception is happening in the following line of code in `OdbcDataReader.cs` from the debugging symbols from System.Data.ODBC module (this is .Net 4.8.1):

```
array[i].scale = (byte)(int)GetColAttribute(i, (ODBC32.SQL_DESC)5, ODBC32.SQL_COLUMN.SCALE, ODBC32.HANDLER.IGNORE);
```

FYI...in GetColAttribute() above, `(ODBC32.SQL_DESC)5 == SQL_COLUMN_SCALE`.

You can see similar code in github here:

https://github.com/microsoft/referencesource/blob/user/rbhanda/NET4.8/System.Data/System/Data/Odbc/OdbcDataReader.cs

```
metaInfos[i].scale = (byte) GetColAttribute(i, (ODBC32.SQL_DESC)ODBC32.SQL_COLUMN.SCALE, ODBC32.SQL_COLUMN.SCALE, ODBC32.HANDLER.IGNORE);
```

In my case, `array[i] = new MetaData();` and MetaData is defined as:

```
private sealed class MetaData
{
    internal int ordinal;
    internal TypeMap typemap;
    internal SQLLEN size;
    internal byte precision;       <------------------------
    internal byte scale;           <------------------------
    internal bool isAutoIncrement;
    internal bool isUnique;
    internal bool isReadOnly;
    internal bool isNullable;
    internal bool isRowVersion;
    internal bool isLong;
    internal bool isKeyColumn;
    internal string baseSchemaName;
    internal string baseCatalogName;
    internal string baseTableName;
    internal string baseColumnName;
}
```
 
Note that precision and scale are both of type `byte`. The return from `GetColAttribute` as seen in the debugger is `0x00000000ffffffff` which is `-1` that is being sent by DuckDB. The value comes from `struct TypeInfo`  defined in api_info.cpp/.hpp.

There is a cast performed `(byte)(int)` but the result of that overflows `array[i].scale` which is type `byte` and exception is thrown. I've tried to replicate this overflow in my own c# test program by casting 0x00000000ffffffff with (byte)(int) and setting it equal to a `byte` but in my case it 'does the right thing' returning 0xff and no overflow occurs. So not sure why System.Data.ODBC has the overflow. 

I'm still trying to track down how the maximum_scale from TypeInfo gets sent to ODBC and why it is 0x00000000ffffffff (an Int32 -1 and not Int64 -1). Also trying to make sense of the overflow as the casting should take care of it. Only thing I can come up with is there is some pointer shenanigans going on. C# in VS2022 comes with training wheels so it is pretty hard to crash...I did try "unsafe {}" block but so far no joy. Also trying to find what a legit value of SQL_COLUMN_SCALE should be...given that scale is type `byte` I don't think -1 is correct as that gets cast to 0xFF.

BTW not the only ODBC driver to have this issue...https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/issues/40654.

Tagging @maiadegraaf 

References
* [SQLColAttribute Function - ODBC API Reference | Microsoft Learn](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/odbc/reference/syntax/sqlcolattribute-function?view=sql-server-ver16)
* [Column Size, Decimal Digits, Transfer Octet Length, Display Size - ODBC API Reference | Microsoft Learn](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/odbc/reference/appendixes/column-size-decimal-digits-transfer-octet-length-and-display-size?view=sql-server-ver16)
* [Decimal Digits - ODBC API Reference | Microsoft Learn](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/odbc/reference/appendixes/decimal-digits?view=sql-server-ver16)

So given my previous...at least for System.Data.ODBC it seems that if we are setting scale we should be sending it a byte. So changing GetColAttribute() in ...\tools\odbc\statement.cpp to the following, swapping SQLCHAR for SQLINTEGER:

```
case SQL_COLUMN_SCALE: {
    if (numeric_attribute_ptr) {
        duckdb::Store<SQLVARCHAR>(desc_record->sql_desc_scale, (duckdb::data_ptr_t)numeric_attribute_ptr);
    }
    return SQL_SUCCESS;
}
```

I've tried this and it does work when using value of -1. However, I'm not sure that -1 is the right value to use here...
